<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Points <m>P</m> on the major arc <m>AB</m> of a circle with centre <m>O</m> all have angle <m>APB = {80}^{\circ}</m>. <m>Q</m> is on the minor arc.</p><p>(a)</p><div>Angle <m>AOB = </m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/> <m>^\circ</m>  </div><p>(b)</p><div>Angle <m>AQB = </m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> <m>^\circ</m>  </div><p>(c) <m>R</m> is on the same side of <m>AB</m> as <m>P</m>, inside the circle. Is angle <m>ARB</m> greater than, equal to or less than <m>{80}^{\circ}</m>?</p><div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>equal to <m>80^\circ</m></li>
<li>greater than <m>80^\circ</m></li>
<li>less than <m>80^\circ</m></li>
</ol>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference: angle <m>AOB = {160}^{\circ}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>APBQ</m> is a cyclic quadrilateral, so its opposite angles add to <m>180^{\circ}</m>: angle <m>AQB = 180^{\circ} - {80}^{\circ} = {100}^{\circ}</m>.</p><p>(c) The arc is the locus of points that see <m>AB</m> at exactly <m>{80}^{\circ}</m>. A point inside the circle, on the same side as the arc, is nearer to <m>AB</m> and sees it at a larger angle: angle <m>ARB</m> is greater than <m>{80}^{\circ}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>160</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>100</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>